---
import MethodSection from "./MethodSection.astro";

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL || "/";
const base = rawBase.endsWith("/") ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;
---

<MethodSection id="problem-framing" title="Why measure against human data" fold>
  <p class="sb-muted sb-measure" slot="lede">
    Critiques of cross-provider homogenization need a ground truth to measure against. Real human
    response distributions provide one.
  </p>

  <div class="sb-prose">
    <p>
      Romasanta, Thomas, and Levina (<a
        href="https://hbr.org/2026/03/researchers-asked-llms-for-strategic-advice-they-got-trendslop-in-return"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">HBR, March 2026</a
      >) ran 15,000 strategic-advice queries across seven frontier LLMs and found that responses
      converged on the same trend-aligned recommendations regardless of scenario context. They called
      this <em>trendslop</em>: a shared attractor across providers, not a quirk of any single model. The
      critique is sound, but it has no measurement behind it. Cross-model consensus is only evidence of
      failure if you can point to the distribution the models should have matched, and open-ended
      strategic questions have no such ground truth.
    </p>
    <p>
      SynthBench supplies that measurement. Every question we evaluate has a human response distribution
      from a real survey: the Pew American Trends Panel, SubPOP subpopulations, GlobalOpinionQA
      cross-country data or the General Social Survey. We score how far each provider's output is from
      that distribution, overall and for each demographic slice.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sb-h3 sb-mh3">Two separate diagnostics</h3>
  <div class="sb-msubs sb-msubs-2">
    <div class="sb-msub">
      <h4 class="sb-h3">Ground-truth fidelity</h4>
      <div class="sb-kicker">Model vs. human distribution</div>
      <p>
        Mean Jensen-Shannon divergence against real survey responses, rolled up into the Survey Parity
        Score (SPS). This is SynthBench's primary metric, and the rest of this page describes how it is
        measured.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sb-msub">
      <h4 class="sb-h3">Cross-provider concordance</h4>
      <div class="sb-kicker">Model vs. model distribution</div>
      <p>
        Pairwise JSD between raw-LLM providers on the same items. Low cross-provider JSD with high
        human-alignment means real population variance is being tracked; low cross-provider JSD with low
        human-alignment is the trendslop signature.
        <a href={`${base}findings/#consensus`}>See how much models agree with each other →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</MethodSection>
